    "title": "<p><strong>Table&nbsp;14.05</strong> &ldquo;Lynch-like syndrome (LLS)&rdquo; is a term used to describe tumours with mismatch repair (MMR) deficiency (and therefore microsatellite instability&nbsp;and/or abnormal immunohistochemistry) due to somatic mutations in both alleles of the same MMR gene, occurring in the setting of other features suggestive of Lynch syndrome (LS), such as young age of onset, other LS-associated tumour types, and a related family history; patients with LLS may have heritable mutations in other (often DNA repair or maintenance) genes that have predisposed them to somatic MMR gene mutations; these are important to recognize and are listed below</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "source error",
